                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The Rosebud Condominium is a fast-paced, hidden object game with a lot of twists and themes to make it challenging, fun and feeling less repetitive. \n\nThe story is about Sarah, she's an architect that got hired by a grieving man to build the tallest building in the world, just because his wife her last words were \"TALLER!\"\nThe sky isn't the limit for her new boss, so she keeps building, even in space.\nShe's also a single mother and doesn't give her daughter any attention while she looses herself in this project. \nEventually she tells her boss she can't build any higher because they are stressing the fabric of space-time, but he's demanding her to keep on building taller!\nThe entire building collapses, they created a big bang and the boss finally sees his dead wife, and she's saying \"Oh silly, That's not what I meant when I said taller!\"\nYou see Sarah with her daughter at her school with a A+ on her test and nothing gets explained any further, which is a little disappointing. \n\nThe game gets harder as you progress and each hidden object is random which gives it a lot of replay value.\nThere are different stages with their own theme and twists to make it more challenging.\nFor example finding things with a flashlight, upside down rooms, rooms that get frozen or locked or aliens flying in front of you to block your view.  \nThis must be the ultimate hidden object game, it's so advanced and it truly impressed me!\nHowever, sometimes the object that you are looking for is standing behind a bigger object, so you can't find it which sucks. \n\nI wouldn't recommend it for the story but the gameplay was challenging and very entertaining. \nWhile normally I turn off the music in games, I kept it on this time because it was very relaxing and fitting. \nToo bad the game doesn't have achievements, it would make the game even better. \n\nApart from a few displeasing factors, my overall experience was great.",
